--- What each mutation shows, and what the probe does not do ---
M1 the pattern kill back: the source check fails. M2 the name match looking for something nothing is called:
the check that *runs the script's own name* against a process named ChatBots fails — that check exists
because the decoy section below it measures the two *rules*, not the script's use of one, and a mutation of
the script cannot be seen by a rule the probe wrote itself. M3 the header stops saying what `--stop` does:
the header check fails.
The real app is never started by the probe — launching it opens a window and an engine on this machine — so
the counterweight is a process renamed to `ChatBots`, which is the same case as far as `pgrep -x` is
concerned. The probe also prints the case it cannot tell apart: a process whose *name* is the bundle path is
matched, because that is also the name the real app has; distinguishing those is not possible by name, and a
same-user process that renames itself is out of scope for a check whose job is to avoid killing unrelated
programs.

